    description: "Spiti is the honest answer to the question of where you can road trip in August without spending the week under cloud. It sits behind the main Himalayan range in a rain shadow, so while Manali is soaked, Kaza is dry, brown and sharp-skied. This is not marketing - it is geography, and it is the single best reason to book a Spiti trip in the monsoon rather than fighting Himachal's wet side.\n\nThe catch is the approach. You still have to get in and out through country that does get rain. Triveni Cabs™ runs the standard circuit Delhi-Shimla-Kinnaur-Kaza-Manali-Delhi, roughly 1,700 km over 8 days, entering via Kinnaur so you gain altitude gradually - Kaza is at about 3,800 m and altitude sickness on a rushed entry is the most common way this trip goes wrong. The Kinnaur gorge is narrow and prone to shooting stones after rain. The Kaza-Manali exit over Kunzum Pass at roughly 4,590 m is rougher still, with unbridged nallah crossings that swell with afternoon meltwater, which is why our drivers cross those early in the day and not at 3 pm. Chandratal and the Kunzum road can shut without notice - we plan it as a bonus, not a promise.\n\nInner Line Permits are required for the border stretch in Kinnaur near Sumdo; carry original ID and passport photos and we handle the Reckong Peo or Kaza paperwork with you. On vehicles: a sedan at ₹11/km can manage the Shimla-Kinnaur-Kaza side, but we do not recommend it for the Kunzum-Batal exit - that stretch wants ground clearance. Innova Crysta at ₹18/km is the sensible choice and works out to around ₹30,600 for the full circuit. A 12-seater Tempo Traveller is ₹23/km, around ₹34,000, and tempo one-way is billed the same as round-trip. Call 7668570551 and we will walk you through which side to enter from.",
    faqs: [
      { question: "Is Spiti Valley open and dry in August?", answer: "Yes, largely. Spiti sits in a rain shadow behind the main Himalayan range, so August there is dry and clear while Manali on the other side is wet. That geography is the whole selling point of an August Spiti trip. The rain risk sits on the approach days through Kinnaur or the Manali side, not in Spiti itself." },
      { question: "Should I enter Spiti via Shimla or Manali?", answer: "Enter via Shimla and Kinnaur, exit via Manali. The Kinnaur route gains altitude gradually over three days, which matters because Kaza sits at roughly 3,800 m and AMS on a rushed entry is the most common problem on this trip. The Manali side puts you over Kunzum Pass at 4,590 m within a day of leaving 2,000 m - fine on the way out, risky on the way in." },
      { question: "Do I need an Inner Line Permit for Spiti?", answer: "Yes, for the Kinnaur border stretch near Sumdo, since it runs close to the international boundary. Permits are issued at Reckong Peo or Kaza - carry original photo ID and passport-size photographs. Indian nationals get them quickly; foreign nationals need a Protected Area Permit and the process is different." },
      { question: "What does a Spiti trip from Delhi cost in August 2026?", answer: "The full Delhi-Shimla-Kinnaur-Kaza-Manali-Delhi circuit runs about 1,700 km over 8 days. That is around ₹18,700 in a sedan at ₹11/km, ₹30,600 in an Innova Crysta at ₹18/km, and ₹34,000 in a 12-seater Tempo Traveller at ₹23/km. We recommend the Innova for this route - the Kunzum exit is not sedan country." },
      { question: "Can we visit Chandratal Lake in August?", answer: "Sometimes. Chandratal and the Kunzum Pass road are the first things to shut when weather turns, and the camps there run a short season. We build it in as an optional day and attempt it if the road is open and dry, but we will not promise it in advance. Treat Chandratal as a bonus." },
      { question: "How rough is the Kaza to Manali road?", answer: "It is the roughest part of the circuit. Kunzum Pass at roughly 4,590 m, then the Batal-Gramphu stretch which is largely unpaved with several unbridged nallah crossings. Those crossings swell with afternoon meltwater, so we cross them in the morning. After Gramphu you drop into Lahaul and take the Atal Tunnel into Manali, which is the easy part." },
      { question: "Is Spiti safe for kids or elderly travellers?", answer: "We would be cautious. Kaza is at 3,800 m, the high circuit villages go above 4,000 m, driving days are long and the nearest full hospital is a long way off. It is not suitable for very young children, anyone with a heart or respiratory condition, or anyone who has had altitude problems before. Be honest with us at booking and we will tell you straight." },
      { question: "Which vehicle is best for Spiti?", answer: "Innova Crysta at ₹18/km is the practical answer - ground clearance for the Kunzum-Batal side and comfortable enough for 1,700 km. Ertiga at ₹15/km works for the Kinnaur side only. Sedans at ₹11/km can reach Kaza via Shimla but we do not recommend the Manali exit in one. Tempo Travellers do run the circuit but need an experienced driver, which is what we send." }
    ]
